Abstract

We present a rigorously grounded control architecture for the Photonically‑Coupled Magnetostructural Skeletal Architecture (PCMSA), a humanoid robot whose structure consists of hollow composite lattice modules linked by passive Halbach magnetic couplings, actuated by embedded Lorentz‑force micro‑coils, and sensed exclusively by differential polymer optical waveguides. The Holonic Mediated Autonomy (HMA) framework is formally defined as a tripartite mediation loop: a policy holon generating unconstrained motion intents, a mediator holon projecting those intents into the feasible set via a layered hierarchy of Control Barrier Functions (CBFs) solved as a Quadratic Program, and a bidirectional teaching channel enabling the policy to internalise constraint geometry. We prove that the PCMSA’s dynamics are control‑affine in coil currents and contact forces, establish a five‑layer CBF chain (angular momentum, DCM containment, self‑collision avoidance, friction cone, and joint‑stroke backstop), and provide a new feasibility theorem showing that the passive magnetic restoring torque guarantees satisfaction of the high‑order joint‑stroke CBF. A photonic extended Kalman filter fusing waveguide curvature and magnetic co‑energy corrects thermal drift, and a short‑horizon mini‑MPC with a disturbance observer ensures input‑to‑state safety under actuator delay. The distributed solver architecture meets a 2 kHz control cycle within 325 µs worst‑case execution time. The central thesis—that the PCMSA’s passive magnetic compliance is a safety resource rather than a control burden—unifies the design, establishing a new paradigm for field‑organised robots.

