1. Introduction

Contemporary humanoid robots inherit a rigid mechanical paradigm: rotary motors, harmonic drives, tendon routings, and bolted assemblies. This architecture imposes a recursive mass escalation and forces a trade‑off between speed and safety. The Photonically‑Coupled Magnetostructural Skeletal Architecture (PCMSA) [1] departs from this tradition by replacing every mechanical transmission with coordinated physical fields. Its bones are hollow carbon‑fibre lattice cylinders, internally braced by angled struts mimicking the pneumatic bones of birds. Modules are connected not by bearings and shafts but by passive Halbach magnetic rings whose strong axial holding force maintains structural integrity without fasteners. Articulation is produced by arrays of Lorentz‑force micro‑coils acting directly in the Halbach gap, and all joint‑angle sensing relies on differential polymer optical waveguides immune to electromagnetic interference.

This radical embodiment solves several problems at once—eliminating gear backlash, reducing mass, and providing inherent backdrivability—but it creates four new control challenges. First, the actuators deliver only ≈12 mN·m of torque per coil, two orders of magnitude below quasi‑direct‑drive motors, requiring coordinated multi‑coil arrays for any macroscopic motion. Second, the Halbach couplings exert strong passive elastic torques and electromagnetic damping forces that are configuration‑dependent and cannot be ignored. Third, the Lorentz actuators have a hard linear stroke limit beyond which the magnetic coupling mechanically decouples, losing structural continuity. Fourth, the photonic waveguide sensors, while immune to electromagnetic noise, drift slowly with temperature.

Conventional whole‑body control (WBC) frameworks—which solve a quadratic program (QP) tracking desired task accelerations under rigid‑body dynamics and friction‑cone constraints [2, 3]—are built for high‑torque, high‑bandwidth actuators and joint encoders. Applying them directly to the PCMSA would saturate the coil currents, excite the passive magnetic dynamics, and risk joint decoupling. What is needed is a control architecture that treats the PCMSA’s physical properties not as obstacles to be overcome but as resources to be exploited.

The central thesis of this paper is that the PCMSA’s passive magnetic compliance is a safety resource, not a control limitation. The elastic restoring torque of the Halbach rings naturally pushes joints toward their elastic centre, assisting any safety filter that prevents stroke‑limit violation. The velocity‑dependent electromagnetic damping slows the divergent component of motion, relaxing the required stability margins. The magnetic gap field itself provides a secondary angle estimate that can be fused with the photonic signal to correct drift. This paper constructs the Holonic Mediated Autonomy (HMA) framework to realise this thesis: a tripartite architecture consisting of a policy holon, a mediator holon, and a bidirectional teaching channel that together enforce whole‑body safety while progressively improving policy behaviour.

The structure of the paper is as follows. Section 2 formally defines the PCMSA body and derives its control‑affine whole‑body dynamics, incorporating the passive magnetic torques as explicit terms. Section 3 introduces the HMA architecture and defines its three components. Section 4 develops the centroidal dynamics and the Divergent Component of Motion (DCM) model adapted to the PCMSA. Section 5 presents the five‑layer hierarchical CBF chain, with particular attention to the second‑order joint‑stroke CBF and a new feasibility theorem exploiting the passive restoring torque. Section 6 describes the photonic state estimator, an extended Kalman filter that fuses waveguide intensity with magnetic co‑energy to compensate thermal drift, and quantifies the safety impact via Projection‑to‑State Safety (PSSf). Section 7 formulates a short‑horizon mini‑MPC with a disturbance observer and proves input‑to‑state safety under bounded actuator delay. Section 8 details the distributed QP solver architecture and its worst‑case execution time analysis. Section 9 provides a discrete‑time safety proof for the coupling decoupling threshold. Section 10 presents the bidirectional teaching protocol. Section 11 integrates the framework with the PCMSA hardware and provides a joint‑assignment map for a 30‑DOF humanoid. Section 12 concludes.

