2. The PCMSA Body: Formal Definition and Dynamics

2.1 Formal Five‑Tuple Definition

A PCMSA robotic body \mathcal{B} is the five‑tuple

\mathcal{B} = (S_m, A_e, C_p, \Phi, \Theta),

where

· S_m = \{M_i\}_{i=1}^{N_s} — hollow composite skeletal modules, each occupying a cylindrical domain \Omega_i \subset \mathbb{R}^3 with orthotropic stiffness \mathbf{C}^{(i)} and density \rho_i(\mathbf{x});
· A_e = \{(\text{coil}_j, \text{magnet}_j, \text{gap}_j)\}_{j=1}^{N_a} — distributed electromagnetic actuators, each consisting of a micro‑coil immersed in the field of an NdFeB Halbach source;
· C_p = \{\text{wg}_l, \text{src}_l, \text{det}_l\}_{l=1}^{N_p} — photonic channels, each a polymer optical waveguide with source‑detector pair;
· \Phi : S_m \times A_e \to \mathbb{R}^3 — the field‑coupling map returning the magnetic flux density at each structural node;
· \Theta : C_p \times S_m \to \mathbb{R}^{N_p} — the photonic readout mapping waveguide deformations to differential optical intensities.

The admissible state space is the set of all configurations (\mathbf{u}, \mathbf{B}_{\text{field}}, \mathbf{I}_{\text{opt}}) satisfying elasticity equilibrium, Maxwell’s equations, and intensity lower bounds, together with the magnetic saturation and noise floor constraints.

2.2 Passive Magnetic Joint Model

Each inter‑module Halbach coupling is formed by two identical coaxial Halbach rings with N_{\text{seg}} magnet segments of remanence B_r = 1.45 T (N52SH grade). The peak gap field at axial separation g is

B_{\text{gap}}(g) = B_r \, \kappa_H(g) \, f_{\text{fill}},

where the enhancement factor for the fundamental harmonic is

\kappa_H(g) = \frac{4}{\pi} \sin\!\left(\frac{\pi}{N_{\text{seg}}}\right) \frac{e^{-g/R_m}}{1 - e^{-2h_m/R_m}} \cdot \cosh\!\left(\frac{g}{2R_m}\right),

with R_m = (R_{\text{outer}}+R_{\text{inner}})/2 the mean ring radius and h_m the magnet thickness. The axial holding force, including a first‑order demagnetisation correction, is

F_{\text{hold}}(g) = \frac{B_{\text{gap}}(g)^2 A_{\text{eff}}}{2\mu_0} \, C_{\text{gap}}(g),
\qquad
C_{\text{gap}}(g) \approx 1 - \frac{\mu_r-1}{\mu_r}\frac{g}{g+h_m}.

For the reference phalanx module (R_m = 10 mm, h_m = 3 mm), F_{\text{hold}}(0) \approx 310 N and F_{\text{hold}}(1\,\text{mm}) \approx 185 N.

Angular misalignment \phi between the two rings generates a restoring torque. To first order in \phi,

\tau_{\text{mag}}(\phi, \dot{\phi}) = -K_\phi(g)\,\phi - D_\phi(g)\,\dot{\phi},

with K_\phi \approx F_{\text{hold}}(g) R_m / 2. At a 1 mm working gap, K_\phi \approx 1.5 N·m/rad. The damping coefficient D_\phi arises from electromagnetic induction when the coil is shunted through a resistor R_d (active damping) and can be tuned to provide critical damping of the elastic mode.

2.3 Actuation Model

Each actuator is a rectangular micro‑coil of N turns, width w, length l, immersed in a local field B_0 = B_{\text{gap}}(g_{\text{act}}). The Lorentz force is

F_L = 2 N I w B_0,

giving a force constant k_F = 2 N w B_0. The coil is placed at a fixed moment arm r from the coupling axis, producing a joint torque

\tau_{\text{act}} = r F_L = \kappa I, \qquad \kappa = 2 N w B_0 r.

For the reference design, with B_0 = 0.8 T, a 2 g coil can produce 0.8 N of force at 1 A, yielding a system force‑to‑mass ratio of approximately 53 N/kg and a peak torque of 12 mN·m. Multiple coils can be arranged in opposing pairs or circumferential arrays to scale the torque; a ten‑coil array achieves about 8 N·m/kg.

2.4 Whole‑Body Dynamics in Control‑Affine Form

Let Q = (q_b, q) \in SE(3) \times \mathbb{R}^n be the floating‑base configuration, where q \in \mathbb{R}^n denotes the n joint angles at the Halbach couplings. The dynamics are

M(Q)\ddot{Q} + C(Q,\dot{Q})\dot{Q} + G(Q) = S^T \bigl(\tau_{\text{passive}} + \tau_{\text{act}}\bigr) + \sum_{c} J_c(Q)^T f_c,

where M is the mass matrix, C the Coriolis/centrifugal term, G the gravity vector, S the joint selection matrix, J_c the contact Jacobian for contact point c, and f_c \in \mathbb{R}^3 the ground reaction force. The passive torque vector is

\tau_{\text{passive}} = -K_p q - D_p \dot{q},

with diagonal positive definite stiffness and damping matrices K_p, D_p. The active torque is \tau_{\text{act}} = K_i \mathbf{i}, where K_i = \operatorname{diag}(\kappa_1,\dots,\kappa_{n_a}) and \mathbf{i} \in \mathbb{R}^{n_a} is the vector of coil currents.

Solving for \ddot{Q} gives the control‑affine form

\ddot{Q} = F(Q,\dot{Q}) + G_u(Q)\, u,

F = -M^{-1}\!\left( C\dot{Q} + G + S^T(K_p q + D_p \dot{q}) \right), \quad
G_u = M^{-1} \big[\, S^T K_i \;\; J_{c_1}^T \;\cdots\; J_{c_k}^T \,\big],

u = \begin{bmatrix} \mathbf{i} \\ f_{c_1} \\ \vdots \\ f_{c_k} \end{bmatrix} \in \mathbb{R}^{n_a + 3k}.

Lemma 2.1 (Control‑Affine Property). The PCMSA dynamics are control‑affine in the compound input u. The control gain matrix G_u is full column rank whenever the robot has at least three non‑coplanar contacts and no kinematic singularity.

This structure is the fundamental enabler of the mediator QP: all safety constraints that are linear in \ddot{Q} become linear in u.

Remark 2.1 (Unmatched Uncertainty in Passive Stiffness). The gap g is not directly sensed; it is inferred from the photonic angle estimate, which has bounded uncertainty \sigma_g. Consequently the passive stiffness is uncertain: K_\phi(g) = K_\phi^{\text{nom}} + \Delta K_\phi, with \Delta K_\phi = |\partial K_\phi/\partial g| \sigma_g. Because this uncertainty multiplies the state q_j rather than the control input, it is an unmatched perturbation. We will account for it by enlarging the safety margins in the HOCBF construction using the Projection‑to‑State Safety framework of Choi et al. [4].

