3. Holonic Mediated Autonomy: Definition and Architecture

Definition 3.1 (Holonic Mediated Autonomy for Robotics).
HMA is a tripartite control architecture consisting of

· a Policy Holon \mathcal{P} that generates unconstrained motion intents u_{\text{AI}};
· a Mediator Holon \mathcal{M} that projects each intent into the feasible set defined by a layered hierarchy of Control Barrier Functions, solved via a warm‑started Quadratic Program; and
· a Teaching Channel \mathcal{T} that propagates structured constraint‑violation experience from \mathcal{M} back to \mathcal{P}, enabling the policy to progressively internalise constraint geometry without retraining.

The term “holon”—an autonomous entity that is simultaneously a whole and a part of a larger whole—was introduced by Koestler and subsequently formalised in manufacturing systems (PROSA architecture) [5, 6]. HMA adapts this concept to robotics: each holon (policy, mediator, teaching) is an autonomous decision unit while functioning as an integral component of the whole‑body control loop. The mediator may recursively contain sub‑holons (e.g., a dedicated thermal‑limit holon, a self‑collision holon) that enforce their respective constraints while presenting a unified safe command to the actuators.

