4. Centroidal Dynamics and the DCM Model

The centroidal momentum G = [P; L] \in \mathbb{R}^6 aggregates the whole‑body linear and angular momentum about the centre of mass. Its rate of change is entirely determined by external forces:

\dot{G} = \begin{bmatrix} m g \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} + \begin{bmatrix} \sum_c f_c \\ \sum_c (p_c - r_{\text{CoM}}) \times f_c \end{bmatrix}.

This equation is independent of the internal coil currents—a critical structural property that decouples the centroidal safety constraints from the actuator limits in the QP.

Under the Linear Inverted Pendulum Model (LIPM) with constant CoM height z_0, the horizontal dynamics simplify to

\ddot{r}_{\text{CoM}}^{xy} = \omega_0^2 ( r_{\text{CoM}}^{xy} - r_{\text{ZMP}} ), \qquad \omega_0 = \sqrt{g/z_0},

where the Zero‑Moment Point is r_{\text{ZMP}} = \frac{\sum_c p_c f_{c,z}}{\sum_c f_{c,z}} (planar approximation). The Divergent Component of Motion

\xi = r_{\text{CoM}}^{xy} + \frac{\dot{r}_{\text{CoM}}^{xy}}{\omega_0}

has first‑order stable dynamics \dot{\xi} = \omega_0 (\xi - r_{\text{ZMP}}), which are affine in the contact forces via the ZMP expression. Thus the DCM flow is control‑affine in u.

The PCMSA’s electromagnetic damping contributes to stabilising the DCM: the effective damping ratio of the CoM motion is increased by approximately 15 %, permitting a corresponding reduction in the DCM safety margin compared to rigid‑actuator robots.

