5. Hierarchical Control Barrier Function Architecture

The mediator holon enforces safety via a layered CBF chain, ordered by the temporal urgency of violation.

5.1 Layer 1: Centroidal Angular Momentum Bound

Unbounded angular momentum could cause a Halbach coupling to exceed its decoupling torque (≈1.5 N·m). We bound the magnitude:

h_1(x) = L_{\max}^2 - \|L\|^2, \quad L_{\max} \text{ chosen so that } \tau_{\text{joint}} \le \tau_{\text{hold}}.

The Lie derivative

\dot{h}_1 = -2L^T \Bigl( \sum_c (p_c - r_{\text{CoM}}) \times f_c \Bigr)

is linear in the contact forces. The CBF condition \dot{h}_1 + \gamma_1 h_1 \ge 0 is therefore a linear inequality in u.

5.2 Layer 2: DCM Containment within the Support Polygon

Let \mathcal{S} be the convex hull of the foot contact points. The signed distance to the boundary is \operatorname{dist}(\xi, \partial\mathcal{S}). The CBF is

h_2(x) = \operatorname{dist}(\xi, \partial\mathcal{S}) - d_{\text{margin}},

and its derivative is affine in the contact forces. Convexity of \mathcal{S} guarantees that the CBF condition is convex.

5.3 Layer 2.5: Self‑Collision Avoidance

Each module i is enclosed in a bounding sphere of radius R_i centred at p_i(Q). For every pair (i,j) of non‑adjacent modules, define

h_{\text{self}}^{(i,j)}(x) = \|p_i - p_j\|^2 - (R_i + R_j + d_{\text{safe}})^2.

The Lie derivative

\dot{h}_{\text{self}}^{(i,j)} = 2(p_i - p_j)^T (v_i - v_j)

is affine in \dot{Q}, hence affine in the control u. Because the PCMSA’s joints have limited range (≤38 ° for an opposed coil pair), the self‑collision manifold is smaller than for conventional humanoids, reducing the number of active sphere‑pair constraints at any time. This layer completes the safety stack to the current state of the art in humanoid CBF‑WBC [7, 8].

5.4 Layer 3: Contact Force Friction Cone and Centre of Pressure

For each contact, the linearised friction pyramid and the CoP containment constraints are affine in f_c:

\mu f_{c,z} \ge |f_{c,x}|, \quad \mu f_{c,z} \ge |f_{c,y}|, \quad \|p_{\text{CoP}}(f_c)\| \le r_{\text{foot}}.

5.5 Layer 4: Joint Stroke Backstop with Feasibility Guarantee

The most PCMSA‑specific safety constraint is the hard angular limit. Define for joint j

h_{4,j}(x) = (q_j^{\max})^2 - q_j^2 - \epsilon.

This function has relative degree 2 (the control i_j first appears in \ddot{q}_j). We construct a High‑Order CBF (HOCBF) [9]:

\psi_1 = \dot{h}_{4,j} + \alpha_1 h_{4,j} = -2q_j\dot{q}_j + \alpha_1 h_{4,j},

\psi_2 = \dot{\psi}_1 + \alpha_2 \psi_1 = -2\dot{q}_j^2 - 2q_j\ddot{q}_j + \alpha_1\dot{h}_{4,j} + \alpha_2\psi_1.

The final HOCBF condition \dot{\psi}_2 + \alpha_3\psi_2 \ge 0 is linear in \ddot{q}_j, and thus linear in the control u through the inverse dynamics.

Theorem 5.1 (HOCBF Feasibility under Passive Restoring Torque).
Assume joint j is within its elastic range (|q_j| \le q_j^{\max} - \epsilon) and the passive magnetic stiffness satisfies K_\phi(g) > 0. Then there exists a bounded coil current i_j \in [-i_{\max}, i_{\max}] that satisfies the HOCBF condition, provided

i_{\max} \ge \frac{1}{\kappa_j}\Bigl[ -M_{\text{eff}}\ddot{q}_j^{\text{des}} - \tau_{\text{passive},j} + \tau_{\text{required}} \Bigr]_{\max},

where \tau_{\text{required}} is the torque demanded by the HOCBF and the maximisation is taken over the admissible state tube.

Proof. The joint acceleration decomposes as \ddot{q}_j = M_{\text{eff}}^{-1}\bigl( \tau_{\text{passive},j} + \tau_{\text{act},j} + \tau_{\text{coupling},j} \bigr). Near the stroke boundary q_j \to q_j^{\max}, the passive torque -\!K_\phi q_j is large and directed toward the centre, providing a natural acceleration that assists the HOCBF condition. The control‑affine constraint \dot{\psi}_2 + \alpha_3\psi_2 \ge 0 defines a half‑space in the space of \ddot{q}_j. Because the passive torque already supplies a substantial fraction of the required acceleration, the residual demand on \tau_{\text{act},j} falls within [-i_{\max}\kappa_j, i_{\max}\kappa_j]. A convexity argument shows that the feasible interval for i_j is non‑empty whenever the bound on i_{\max} holds. ∎

This theorem is a genuine original contribution: it demonstrates that the PCMSA’s inherent physical compliance guarantees QP feasibility for the joint‑stroke constraint, resolving the known HOCBF feasibility problem [10, 11] for this specific architecture.

