6. Photonic State Estimation with Drift‑Compensated Safety

6.1 Differential Waveguide Model

Each rotational degree of freedom is monitored by two polymer waveguides bonded to opposite faces of the module. The differential intensity is

\Delta I_j = I_j^{(+)} - I_j^{(-)} \approx S_j q_j + b_j(T),

where S_j = 0.12 dB/deg for the phalanx module and b_j(T) is a temperature‑dependent bias. The waveguide and the coil are co‑located, so the coil temperature T_{\text{coil}} serves as a drift correlate.

6.2 Magnetic Co‑Energy Angle Estimate

The Halbach gap field B_{\text{gap}}(g,\phi) provides an independent joint‑angle observation. Inverting the field model yields

\hat{\phi}_{\text{mag}} = f_{\text{mag}}(B_{\text{gap}}, g),

available at 200 Hz. This measurement is immune to optical drift but has a coarser resolution.

6.3 Extended Kalman Filter with PSSf Margin Adaptation

We fuse the two sources via an extended Kalman filter. State: x = [q_j, \dot{q}_j, b_j]^T. Prediction uses the rigid‑body dynamics; the measurement update is

z_k = \begin{bmatrix} \Delta I_j \\ \hat{\phi}_{\text{mag}} \end{bmatrix},\qquad
H_k = \begin{bmatrix} S_j & 0 & 1 \\ \displaystyle\frac{\partial f_{\text{mag}}}{\partial q}\Big|_{\hat{q}_j} & 0 & 0 \end{bmatrix}.

The Jacobian \partial f_{\text{mag}}/\partial q is computed online from the gap field gradient model and varies by ±12 % across the elastic range; the process noise Q_k is inflated accordingly. The bias evolves as \dot{b}_j = -\lambda (b_j - b_\infty(T_{\text{coil}})) + w, with white process noise w.

The EKF outputs a joint‑angle estimate with covariance \sigma_j^2. Because the stiffness uncertainty \Delta K_\phi is a projected (state‑dependent) rather than input‑matched disturbance, we employ the Projection‑to‑State Safety (PSSf) framework [4] to translate the estimation error into a required CBF margin enlargement: when \sigma_j increases, the mediator automatically widens the stroke margin by a factor \propto \sigma_j, maintaining formal safety under photonic drift.

