7. Short‑Horizon Safety Preview with Disturbance‑Aware Stability

7.1 Motivation and Mini‑MPC Formulation

A pure instantaneous CBF does not account for the 2–3 ms transport delay between coil current command and force buildup. We therefore solve a mini‑MPC over a horizon K = 3 steps (1.5 ms at 2 kHz):

\min_{\{u[k]\}_{k=0}^{K-1}} \sum_{k=0}^{K-1} \left( \|u[k] - u_{\text{AI}}\|_W^2 + \lambda \|\Delta u[k]\|^2 \right),

subject to the linearised CBF constraints on predicted states

x[k+1] = x[k] + \Delta t \bigl( f(x[k]) + g(x[k]) u[k] \bigr)

and the actuator bounds. Only the first control u[0] is executed.

7.2 Disturbance Observer

External contacts (tool grasping, surface touch) produce impulsive forces that the mini‑MPC’s open‑loop model does not predict. A momentum‑based disturbance observer [12] runs at 2 kHz, estimating the external torque \hat{\tau}_{\text{ext}} from the generalised momentum residual:

\hat{\tau}_{\text{ext}} = K_O \left( p - \int_0^t (\tau + C^T\dot{Q} - G + \hat{\tau}_{\text{ext}})\,ds \right),

where p = M\dot{Q} is the generalised momentum. This estimate is fed into the mini‑MPC’s prediction model, improving contact detection and reducing CBF margin conservatism.

7.3 ISSf Safety Under Delay

Lemma 7.1 (Lipschitz continuity of the HOCBF chain). On the compact set \mathcal{D}_j = \{|q_j| \le q_j^{\max} - \epsilon,\; |\dot{q}_j| \le \dot{q}_{\max}\}, the Lie derivatives of the HOCBF functions \psi_1, \psi_2 are Lipschitz continuous.

Proof. The passive stiffness K_\phi(g) is smooth on any compact gap interval. All terms in \dot{\psi}_2 are polynomials and exponentials of bounded arguments, hence Lipschitz. ∎

Theorem 7.2 (ISSf under bounded delay). If the actuator delay satisfies \tau \le \Delta t = 500 µs and the linearised prediction error is bounded, the closed‑loop system is input‑to‑state safe: there exists a class‑\mathcal{K} function \varepsilon such that h(x(t)) \ge -\varepsilon(\tau) for all t.

Proof sketch. Lemma 7.1 guarantees that the CBF condition evaluated on the predicted state differs from the true future state by a bounded amount. The ISSf result of Kolathaya and Ames [13] then ensures that the system remains within an enlarged safe set whose radius is proportional to the disturbance bound. ∎

