8. QP Solver Architecture and Real‑Time Implementation

8.1 Solver Formulation

The mediator solves at each control cycle

\begin{aligned}
\min_{u,\delta} \quad & \frac12 u^T H u + g^T u + \frac{\rho}{2}\|\delta\|^2 \\
\text{s.t.} \quad & lbA \le A u - \delta \le ubA,\\
& u_{\min} \le u \le u_{\max},\quad \delta \ge 0,
\end{aligned}

where g = -u_{\text{AI}} penalises deviation from the AI intent. The slack vector \delta has a strict priority hierarchy: Layer 4 (stroke) and Layer 2.5 (self‑collision) are always hard (\delta_k = 0), while lower layers are softened sequentially.

8.2 Solver Selection and Timing

We adopt the qpSWIFT sparse active‑set solver [14], which achieves consistent average solve times of ≈0.2 ms on humanoid WBC problems [7] and exhibits tighter worst‑case timing than earlier active‑set methods. Constraint evaluation is GPU‑accelerated on the central compute module (NVIDIA Jetson Thor). The worst‑case execution time for 32 variables and 50 active constraints is:

· Photonic state estimator (FPGA, DMA read): 0 µs added latency (data are double‑buffered; one‑cycle staleness of 500 µs introduces ≤0.03° uncertainty, accounted for by the CBF margin).
· CBF evaluation (GPU): 70–100 µs.
· QP assembly: 50–70 µs.
· qpSWIFT warm‑start solve: 90–130 µs.
· Teaching signal generation: 15–25 µs.
· Total: 225–325 µs, comfortably within the 500 µs budget for 2 kHz control.

8.3 Distributed Hardware

Coil‑driver microcontrollers communicate over EtherCAT with DC‑mode synchronisation at a 500 µs cycle. The photonic interrogator streams intensity data to the central module over a dedicated Gigabit Ethernet link. Double‑buffered state and command arrays enable pipelined execution: the next cycle’s CBF evaluation begins while the current QP is being solved.

