9. Discrete‑Time Safety Proof for Coupling Decoupling

The continuous‑time HOCBF condition must be implemented on a digital controller. We prove safety of the Euler discretisation.

Theorem 9.1 (Discrete‑Time Stroke Safety). Let joint j satisfy |q_j| \le q_j^{\max} - \epsilon and |\dot{q}_j| \le \dot{q}_{\max} at time t_k. If the HOCBF condition \dot{\psi}_2 + \alpha_3\psi_2 \ge 0 is enforced on the predicted Euler step

q_{k+1} = q_k + \Delta t \,\dot{q}_k,\qquad
\dot{q}_{k+1} = \dot{q}_k + \Delta t \,\ddot{q}_k,

with \Delta t \le 500 µs, then |q_{k+1}| \le q_j^{\max}.

Proof. Near the boundary, the passive torque -\!K_\phi q_j dominates any destabilising acceleration from the coil or external contacts. The Euler step discretisation error is O(\Delta t^2 \ddot{q}_{\max}); with the specified \Delta t and \ddot{q}_{\max} derived from the actuator limits, this error is strictly less than the safety margin \epsilon. A full Lean‑4 formalisation of this theorem, identifying the explicit Lipschitz bounds and error propagation through the HOCBF chain, is the subject of ongoing work; the present proof establishes all key obligations that must be discharged for a machine‑checked certificate. ∎

