12. Conclusion

The PCMSA humanoid robot represents a paradigm shift in robotic embodiment: every mechanical transmission is replaced by a coordinated physical field. This paper has constructed the Holonic Mediated Autonomy framework to control such a field‑organised body, and in doing so has demonstrated a deeper principle: passive physical compliance, when properly modelled and respected, can be a safety resource rather than a control burden. The five‑layer CBF chain—centroidal angular momentum bound, DCM containment, self‑collision avoidance, friction cone, and joint‑stroke backstop—together with the HOCBF feasibility theorem, the photonic EKF with PSSf margin adaptation, and the ISSf‑guaranteed mini‑MPC, provide a complete, provably safe whole‑body control solution tailored to the PCMSA’s unique physics. The bidirectional teaching channel ensures that AI policies can progressively internalise these constraints, making the architecture suitable for autonomous deployment in unstructured environments. The next step is multiphysics simulation and experimental validation of a three‑module finger, which will be reported in a companion paper.

