Appendix A: PCMSA Structural and Magnetic Module Specifications

A.1 Hollow Lattice Module Geometry

Each skeletal module M_i is a hollow cylinder of length L_i, outer radius R_{\text{outer}}, and inner radius R_{\text{inner}}. The hollowness ratio is \eta = (R_{\text{inner}}/R_{\text{outer}})^2. The wall thickness is t_c = R_{\text{outer}} - R_{\text{inner}}. The wall consists of a symmetric [0^\circ/90^\circ]_{\text{s}} cross‑ply laminate of unidirectional carbon‑fibre‑reinforced polymer (CFRP) with fibre volume fraction V_f = 0.6. The homogenised orthotropic engineering constants are:

· Axial modulus E_L = 135 GPa
· Hoop modulus E_T = 10 GPa
· In‑plane shear modulus G_{LT} = 5 GPa
· Major Poisson ratio \nu_{LT} = 0.25
· Density \rho_c = 1570 kg·m⁻³

The internal lattice consists of N_{\text{strut}} slender CFRP rods of cross‑sectional area A_{\text{strut}} and radius r_s, crossing the core at angles \theta_k \in [25^\circ, 60^\circ] to the module axis. The struts are bonded to the inner wall and provide torsional and bending reinforcement. The mass of one module is

m_i = \rho_c \pi (R_{\text{outer}}^2 - R_{\text{inner}}^2) L_i + N_{\text{strut}} \rho_c A_{\text{strut}} \frac{2R_{\text{inner}}}{\sin\theta_k} + 2 m_{\text{Halbach}} + m_{\text{coils}} + m_{\text{wg}},

where m_{\text{Halbach}} = \rho_{\text{mag}} \pi (R_m^2 - R_{m,\text{in}}^2) h_m with magnet density \rho_{\text{mag}} = 7500 kg·m⁻³.

The phalanx module (reference design) has L_i = 60 mm, D_{\text{outer}} = 18 mm, \eta = 0.85, giving wall thickness t_c = 1.35 mm and inner radius R_{\text{inner}} = 7.65 mm. The total module mass is approximately 29.5 g.

A.2 Halbach Ring Magnetic Model

Each coupling consists of two identical coaxial Halbach rings with N_{\text{seg}} magnet segments, remanence B_r = 1.45 T (N52SH), thickness h_m = 3 mm, mean radius R_m = 10 mm, and inner radius R_{m,\text{in}} = 6 mm. The ring magnetization pattern rotates by 2\pi/N_{\text{seg}} per segment, producing a strongly augmented field on the inner (gap) side and near cancellation on the outer side.

The magnetic scalar potential in the air gap satisfies Laplace's equation with boundary conditions given by the prescribed magnetization \mathbf{M}(\mathbf{x}). For an ideal continuous Halbach cylinder of infinite axial extent, the field inside the bore is uniform. For a finite segmented ring, retaining the fundamental harmonic gives the axial flux density at the median plane z = g/2 as

B_z(r,z) = B_r \frac{4}{\pi} \sin\!\left(\frac{\pi}{N_{\text{seg}}}\right) \frac{\cosh(z/R_m)}{\sinh(h_m/R_m)} e^{-(r - R_m)/R_m},

where the radial coordinate r is measured from the ring centre. At the gap centre (z=0, r=R_m) the peak field becomes

B_{\text{gap}}(g) = B_r \,\kappa_H(g)\, f_{\text{fill}},

\kappa_H(g) = \frac{4}{\pi} \sin\!\left(\frac{\pi}{N_{\text{seg}}}\right) \frac{e^{-g/R_m}}{1 - e^{-2h_m/R_m}} \cosh\!\left(\frac{g}{2R_m}\right),

and the fill factor f_{\text{fill}} \approx 0.95 accounts for gaps between segments. For N_{\text{seg}} = 12, h_m = 3 mm, R_m = 8 mm (finger‑scale ring), the enhancement factor is \kappa_H(0) = 1.42 at contact and \kappa_H(1\,\text{mm}) = 1.21.

A.3 Holding Force and Angular Stiffness

The axial holding force is derived from the Maxwell stress tensor:

F_{\text{hold}}(g) = \frac{B_{\text{gap}}(g)^2 A_{\text{eff}}}{2\mu_0} \, C_{\text{gap}}(g),

C_{\text{gap}}(g) \approx 1 - \frac{\mu_r - 1}{\mu_r} \frac{g}{g + h_m},

with \mu_r \approx 1.05 for NdFeB. For the phalanx ring dimensions (R_m = 10 mm, R_{m,\text{in}} = 6 mm): F_{\text{hold}}(0) \approx 310 N, F_{\text{hold}}(1\,\text{mm}) \approx 185 N.

Angular misalignment \phi produces a restoring torque

\tau_{\text{mag}} = -K_\phi(g) \phi, \qquad K_\phi(g) \approx \frac{F_{\text{hold}}(g) R_m}{2}.

At g = 1 mm, K_\phi \approx 1.5 N·m/rad. The electromagnetic damping coefficient when the coil is shunted by a resistor R_d is

D_\phi(g) = \frac{(2 N w B_0(g))^2}{R_c + R_d},

with R_c the coil resistance. For the reference coil (N=50, w=10 mm, B_0=0.8 T, R_c=2\ \Omega, R_d=10\ \Omega), D_\phi \approx 0.02 N·m·s/rad.

A.4 Actuator Force‑to‑Mass and Torque Density

The Lorentz force on a coil of N turns, each of active length w in field B_0, carrying current I is

F_L = 2 N w B_0 I = k_F I, \qquad k_F = 2 N w B_0.

The coil mass is m_{\text{coil}} \approx 2 g. The Halbach source mass providing the field is m_{\text{mag}} \approx 11.3 g. The total actuator mass is m_{\text{act}} \approx 15 g. At thermally limited current I = 1 A, F_L = 0.8 N, giving a system force‑to‑mass ratio

\Gamma_{\text{sys}} = \frac{0.8\ \text{N}}{0.015\ \text{kg}} \approx 53\ \text{N·kg}^{-1}.

With a refined design (optimised coil fill factor, 1.2 A peak), this rises to 80–180 N·kg⁻¹. The torque about the coupling axis, with moment arm r = 15 mm, is

\tau = r F_L = 0.012\ \text{N·m} = 12\ \text{mN·m},

giving a torque density of 0.8 N·m/kg for a single coil. An array of ten coils operating in parallel yields approximately 8 N·m/kg.

A.5 Joint Stroke and Decoupling Limits

The maximum linear stroke of the coil in the Halbach gap is s_{\max} = 5 mm. The corresponding joint angle range for a single coil is

\Delta q = \frac{s_{\max}}{r} = \frac{5}{15} = 0.333\ \text{rad} \approx 19^\circ.

An opposed coil pair in push‑pull configuration doubles the stroke to 10 mm, yielding a maximum angular range of \approx 38^\circ. Beyond this limit, the coil leaves the region of strong magnetic field and the Halbach rings mechanically decouple, acting as an overload release mechanism. The decoupling torque threshold is \tau_{\text{dec}} \approx K_\phi q_{\max} \approx 1.5 \times 0.33 = 0.5 N·m, well above the continuous operating torque.

A.6 Demagnetisation and Thermal Safety Margins

The N52SH grade has intrinsic coercivity H_{cj} \ge 20 kOe (1592 kA/m) and a maximum continuous operating temperature of 150^\circC. The coil temperature is bounded at 80^\circC by the thermal management system, giving a \Delta T = 70^\circC margin against irreversible thermal demagnetisation. Mechanical shock demagnetisation requires stresses exceeding the magnet's fracture limit; the rings are embedded within the CFRP shell, which provides mechanical constraint. Under a 1 m drop, the estimated peak deceleration of 100\,g produces an interfacial stress well below the NdFeB compressive strength of \sim 1000 MPa.

