Appendix B: Derivation of the Whole‑Body Control‑Affine Dynamics

B.1 Floating‑Base Rigid‑Body Dynamics

The PCMSA is modelled as a floating‑base tree structure with n internal degrees of freedom. The generalised coordinates are Q = (q_b, q) \in SE(3) \times \mathbb{R}^n, where q_b represents the position and orientation of the base link and q are the joint angles at the Halbach couplings. The Lagrangian equations of motion are

M(Q)\ddot{Q} + C(Q,\dot{Q})\dot{Q} + G(Q) = \tau_{\text{gen}},

with mass matrix M(Q) \succ 0, Coriolis and centrifugal term C(Q,\dot{Q})\dot{Q} satisfying \dot{M} - 2C skew‑symmetric, and gravity vector G(Q) = \partial U/\partial Q.

The generalised forces \tau_{\text{gen}} consist of:

· Passive magnetic torques at the joints: S^T \tau_{\text{passive}},
· Active coil torques: S^T \tau_{\text{act}},
· External contact forces: \sum_c J_c(Q)^T f_c,

where S = [0_{n \times 6} \;\; I_{n \times n}] selects the joint subspace. Thus

M\ddot{Q} + C\dot{Q} + G = S^T(\tau_{\text{passive}} + \tau_{\text{act}}) + \sum_c J_c^T f_c.

B.2 Passive and Active Joint Torques

The passive torque at coupling j is

\tau_{\text{passive},j} = -K_{\phi}(g_j) q_j - D_{\phi}(g_j) \dot{q}_j,

where the gap g_j is itself a function of the joint configuration: for a revolute coupling with axial offset, g_j = g_{j,0} + \delta g(q_j). The stiffness and damping are locally linearised around the nominal gap. Assembling all joints gives the vector form

\tau_{\text{passive}} = -K_p(q) q - D_p(q) \dot{q},

with K_p, D_p positive definite and configuration‑dependent.

The active torque is the product of the coil current and the motor constant:

\tau_{\text{act},j} = \kappa_j i_j, \qquad \kappa_j = 2 N_j w_j B_{0,j} r_j,

where B_{0,j} = B_{\text{gap}}(g_j) is the local field at the coil location. In vector form, \tau_{\text{act}} = K_i \mathbf{i} with K_i = \operatorname{diag}(\kappa_1,\dots,\kappa_{n_a}).

B.3 Control‑Affine Form

Define the generalised velocity \dot{Q} and acceleration \ddot{Q}. The passive terms are absorbed into the drift:

M\ddot{Q} + (C\dot{Q} + G + S^T K_p q + S^T D_p \dot{q}) = S^T K_i \mathbf{i} + \sum_c J_c^T f_c.

Solving for \ddot{Q}:

\ddot{Q} = -M^{-1}\bigl(C\dot{Q} + G + S^T(K_p q + D_p \dot{q})\bigr) + M^{-1}\bigl(S^T K_i \mathbf{i} + \sum_c J_c^T f_c\bigr).

Setting

F(Q,\dot{Q}) = -M^{-1}\bigl(C\dot{Q} + G + S^T(K_p q + D_p \dot{q})\bigr),

G_u(Q) = M^{-1}\bigl[ S^T K_i \;\; J_{c_1}^T \;\; \cdots \;\; J_{c_k}^T \bigr],

u = \begin{bmatrix} \mathbf{i} \\ f_{c_1} \\ \vdots \\ f_{c_k} \end{bmatrix} \in \mathbb{R}^{n_a + 3k},

the dynamics take the standard control‑affine form

\ddot{Q} = F(Q,\dot{Q}) + G_u(Q)\, u.

The matrix G_u is full column rank when the robot is not in a kinematically singular configuration and at least three non‑coplanar contacts exist, ensuring independent control authority over the joint accelerations and contact forces.

B.4 Unmatched Uncertainty Model

The gap g_j is not directly measured; it is estimated from the photonic angle via the joint kinematics. The estimation uncertainty \sigma_g propagates into the stiffness and damping terms as unmatched uncertainty:

K_p(q) = K_p^{\text{nom}}(q) + \Delta K_p, \qquad \Delta K_p = \frac{\partial K_p}{\partial g} \sigma_g.

This uncertainty multiplies the state q rather than the control input, necessitating a robust safety framework such as Projection‑to‑State Safety (see Appendix E).

