Appendix C: Centroidal Dynamics and DCM Model

C.1 Centroidal Momentum

The robot is composed of N_b rigid bodies of mass m_i, centre‑of‑mass position r_i, velocity v_i, inertia tensor I_i, and angular velocity \omega_i. The total linear and angular momenta about the overall centre of mass r_{\text{CoM}} are

P = \sum_i m_i v_i, \qquad
L = \sum_i \bigl( (r_i - r_{\text{CoM}}) \times m_i v_i + I_i \omega_i \bigr).

The centroidal momentum vector is G = [P; L] \in \mathbb{R}^6. Its rate of change equals the net external wrench (Newton‑Euler):

\dot{G} = \begin{bmatrix} m g \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} + \begin{bmatrix} \sum_c f_c \\ \sum_c (p_c - r_{\text{CoM}}) \times f_c \end{bmatrix},

where m = \sum_i m_i is the total mass. Crucially, the internal actuation forces (both passive and active) cancel and do not appear; \dot{G} is affine in the contact forces \{f_c\} alone.

C.2 Linear Inverted Pendulum Model

Under the LIPM, the CoM height is constrained to a constant value z_0. The horizontal acceleration of the CoM satisfies

\ddot{r}_{\text{CoM}}^{xy} = \omega_0^2 \bigl( r_{\text{CoM}}^{xy} - r_{\text{ZMP}} \bigr), \qquad \omega_0 = \sqrt{\frac{g}{z_0}}.

The Zero‑Moment Point is a function of the contact forces and their locations. For a flat foot with coplanar contacts,

r_{\text{ZMP}} = \frac{\sum_c p_c f_{c,z}}{\sum_c f_{c,z}},

which is affine in the contact forces (with normalisation). Thus the CoM acceleration is control‑affine in the same u as the full dynamics.

C.3 Divergent Component of Motion

The LIPM dynamics are a second‑order linear system with one unstable mode. The divergent component of motion (DCM) is defined as

\xi = r_{\text{CoM}}^{xy} + \frac{\dot{r}_{\text{CoM}}^{xy}}{\omega_0},

and its time derivative is

\dot{\xi} = \dot{r}_{\text{CoM}}^{xy} + \frac{\ddot{r}_{\text{CoM}}^{xy}}{\omega_0} = \omega_0 (\xi - r_{\text{ZMP}}).

This is a first‑order system, stable if the ZMP is placed beyond the DCM. The DCM must be maintained within the convex support polygon \mathcal{S} to prevent foot tipping. The signed distance function \operatorname{dist}(\xi, \partial\mathcal{S}) is concave on \mathcal{S} (since \mathcal{S} is convex), making the corresponding CBF condition convex.

