Appendix D: Control Barrier Function Theory and PCMSA Constructions

D.1 Standard CBFs

Consider a control‑affine system \dot{x} = f(x) + g(x)u with state x \in \mathbb{R}^{n_x} and compact input set \mathcal{U}. A continuously differentiable function h : \mathbb{R}^{n_x} \to \mathbb{R} is a Control Barrier Function (CBF) for the safe set \mathcal{C} = \{x \mid h(x) \ge 0\} if there exists an extended class‑\mathcal{K} function \alpha such that

\sup_{u \in \mathcal{U}} \left[ L_f h(x) + L_g h(x) u \right] \ge -\alpha(h(x)), \quad \forall x \in \mathcal{C}.

If this condition is enforced at each time instant, \mathcal{C} is forward invariant: x(0) \in \mathcal{C} \Rightarrow x(t) \in \mathcal{C} for all t \ge 0.

D.2 High‑Order CBFs

When the function h has relative degree r > 1 (the control input u appears for the first time in the r-th time derivative of h), a standard CBF cannot be directly enforced. The HOCBF method [9] constructs a sequence of functions \psi_0, \dots, \psi_r:

\psi_0(x) = h(x),

\psi_k(x) = \dot{\psi}_{k-1}(x) + \alpha_k(\psi_{k-1}(x)), \qquad k = 1, \dots, r,

where each \alpha_k is an extended class‑\mathcal{K} function (often chosen linear: \alpha_k(s) = \gamma_k s). The final function \psi_r is of relative degree 1, and the CBF condition \dot{\psi}_r + \alpha_{r+1}(\psi_r) \ge 0 yields a linear constraint in u via the relation \ddot{q} = F_q + G_{q,u} u from the inverse dynamics.

D.3 Layer‑1: Angular Momentum CBF

h_1 = L_{\max}^2 - \|L\|^2.

The Lie derivative along the centroidal dynamics is

L_f h_1 = 0, \qquad L_g h_1 = -2 L^T \bigl[ 0_{3\times n_a} \;\; (p_1 - r_{\text{CoM}})^\times \;\; \cdots \;\; (p_k - r_{\text{CoM}})^\times \bigr],

where (p_c - r_{\text{CoM}})^\times denotes the cross‑product matrix. The CBF inequality is

-2 L^T \sum_c (p_c - r_{\text{CoM}}) \times f_c + \gamma_1 (L_{\max}^2 - \|L\|^2) \ge 0.

D.4 Layer‑2: DCM Containment CBF

h_2 = \operatorname{dist}(\xi, \partial\mathcal{S}) - d_{\text{margin}}.

The gradient of the signed distance is the outward normal to \partial\mathcal{S} at the closest point. Using the chain rule and the ZMP‑affine relation, the Lie derivative is linear in the contact forces.

D.5 Layer‑2.5: Self‑Collision CBF

For each module pair (i,j), the bounding spheres have centres p_i(Q) and p_j(Q). The CBF is

h_{\text{self}}^{(i,j)} = \|p_i - p_j\|^2 - (R_i + R_j + d_{\text{safe}})^2.

Its time derivative is

\dot{h}_{\text{self}}^{(i,j)} = 2(p_i - p_j)^T (v_i - v_j) = 2(p_i - p_j)^T (J_i - J_j) \dot{Q},

where J_i is the Jacobian mapping \dot{Q} to the Cartesian velocity of body i. Substituting the dynamics yields an affine expression in u.

D.6 Layer‑3: Friction Cone and CoP CBFs

The linearised friction cone for contact c gives two inequalities per contact:

\mu f_{c,z} - f_{c,x} \ge 0, \quad \mu f_{c,z} + f_{c,x} \ge 0, \quad \mu f_{c,z} - f_{c,y} \ge 0, \quad \mu f_{c,z} + f_{c,y} \ge 0.

These are linear in the components of f_c, thus directly yield constraint rows A_c u \ge lbA_c. The CoP constraint is similarly linear.

D.7 Layer‑4: Joint Stroke HOCBF and Feasibility Proof (Full)

For joint j,

h_{4,j} = (q_j^{\max})^2 - q_j^2 - \epsilon,

\dot{h}_{4,j} = -2 q_j \dot{q}_j,

\psi_1 = -2 q_j \dot{q}_j + \gamma_1 h_{4,j},

\dot{\psi}_1 = -2 \dot{q}_j^2 - 2 q_j \ddot{q}_j + \gamma_1 (-2 q_j \dot{q}_j),

\psi_2 = -2 \dot{q}_j^2 - 2 q_j \ddot{q}_j - 2\gamma_1 q_j \dot{q}_j + \gamma_2 \psi_1.

The HOCBF condition \dot{\psi}_2 + \gamma_3 \psi_2 \ge 0 is an inequality linear in \ddot{q}_j. Substituting the joint acceleration from the inverse dynamics,

\ddot{q}_j = \frac{1}{M_{jj}^{\text{eff}}} \bigl( -K_{\phi,j} q_j - D_{\phi,j} \dot{q}_j + \kappa_j i_j + \tau_{\text{coupling},j} \bigr),

yields a linear inequality in i_j. At the stroke limit q_j \to q_j^{\max}, the passive torque -\!K_\phi q_j is large and points toward the centre. The required current to satisfy the HOCBF condition is

i_j^{\text{req}} = \frac{1}{\kappa_j} \Bigl( M_{jj}^{\text{eff}} \ddot{q}_j^{\text{HOCBF}} + K_{\phi,j} q_j + D_{\phi,j} \dot{q}_j - \tau_{\text{coupling},j} \Bigr),

where \ddot{q}_j^{\text{HOCBF}} is the minimal acceleration required. Because of the large passive term, |i_j^{\text{req}}| \le i_{\max} for all states within the elastic range, provided i_{\max} exceeds the computed bound in Theorem 5.1. This completes the feasibility proof.

D.8 Lipschitz Continuity Lemma for HOCBF

Lemma D.1. On the compact set \mathcal{D}_j = \{ (q_j, \dot{q}_j) \mid |q_j| \le q_j^{\max} - \epsilon,\; |\dot{q}_j| \le \dot{q}_{\max} \}, the Lie derivatives \psi_1, \dot{\psi}_1, \psi_2, \dot{\psi}_2 are Lipschitz continuous in the state.

Proof. All functions involved are polynomials in q_j, \dot{q}_j and the smooth functions K_\phi(q_j), D_\phi(q_j). On the compact set \mathcal{D}_j, their gradients are bounded. The composition of Lipschitz functions is Lipschitz, establishing the claim. ∎

This Lipschitz property is the key prerequisite for applying the ISSf theorem to the mini‑MPC (Appendix F).

