Appendix E: Photonic Extended Kalman Filter with Drift Compensation

E.1 Waveguide Bending‑Loss Model

A multimode polymer waveguide (core: PDMS, n_{\text{core}} = 1.41; cladding: fluorinated polymer, n_{\text{clad}} = 1.38; core diameter 300 µm, NA = 0.28) has baseline propagation loss \alpha_0 = 35 dB/m at 850 nm. Under local curvature \kappa(s), the additional bending loss is

\alpha_{\text{bend}}(s) = C_1 \exp\!\bigl(-C_2 / \kappa(s)\bigr),

with C_1 \approx 100 dB/m, C_2 \approx 0.1 m⁻¹. For small curvatures, this is approximately linear. Two waveguides are bonded to opposite faces of the module (offset \pm d from the neutral plane). The differential transmitted intensity for joint j is

\Delta I_j = I_0 \Bigl[ e^{-\int_{\Gamma_j^+} (\alpha_0 + \alpha_{\text{bend}}) ds} - e^{-\int_{\Gamma_j^-} (\alpha_0 + \alpha_{\text{bend}}) ds} \Bigr] \approx S_j q_j + b_j(T),

with sensitivity S_j \approx 0.12 dB/deg (phalanx module).

E.2 Magnetic Co‑Energy Angle Estimate

The Halbach gap field magnitude depends on the relative pose of the two rings. The gap g and angular misalignment \phi determine the flux density via the model of Section A.2. Inverting this relationship for small \phi yields

\hat{\phi}_{\text{mag}} = f_{\text{mag}}(B_{\text{gap}}, g) = \frac{1}{c_1(g)} \left( \frac{B_{\text{gap}}}{B_r} - c_0(g) \right),

where c_0(g), c_1(g) are configuration‑dependent coefficients derived from the field expansion. The Jacobian \partial f_{\text{mag}}/\partial \phi = 1/c_1(g) is computed online from a lookup table parameterised by the estimated gap.

E.3 EKF State and Prediction

State vector: x = [q_j, \dot{q}_j, b_j]^T. The continuous‑time prediction model is

\dot{q}_j = \dot{q}_j,

\ddot{q}_j = \frac{1}{M_{\text{eff}}} \bigl( -K_\phi q_j - D_\phi \dot{q}_j + \kappa_j i_j + \tau_{\text{coupling},j} \bigr),

\dot{b}_j = -\lambda (b_j - b_\infty(T_{\text{coil}})).

Discretisation at \Delta t = 100 µs (10 kHz photonic loop) gives x_{k+1} = f_d(x_k, u_k) + w_k, where w_k \sim \mathcal{N}(0, Q_k).

E.4 Measurement Update

Two measurements arrive asynchronously:

· Photonic differential intensity at 10 kHz: z_{1,k} = S_j q_k + b_k + v_{1,k}.
· Magnetic angle estimate at 200 Hz: z_{2,k} = q_k + \frac{1}{c_1(g_k)}( \frac{B_{\text{gap},k}}{B_r} - c_0(g_k) ) + v_{2,k}.

Linearising the magnetic measurement about the predicted state \hat{x}_{k|k-1}:

H_{2,k} = \left[ \frac{\partial f_{\text{mag}}}{\partial q}\Big|_{\hat{q}_{k|k-1}} \;\; 0 \;\; 0 \right].

The Jacobian varies by ±12 % across the elastic range; this scheduling error is compensated by inflating the corresponding element of the process noise covariance Q_k.

The Kalman gain is computed in the standard way:

K_k = P_{k|k-1} H_k^T (H_k P_{k|k-1} H_k^T + R_k)^{-1},

\hat{x}_{k|k} = \hat{x}_{k|k-1} + K_k (z_k - h(\hat{x}_{k|k-1})),

P_{k|k} = (I - K_k H_k) P_{k|k-1}.

The estimated covariance P_{k|k} provides the uncertainty \sigma_j^2 = P_{11} used in the PSSf margin adaptation.

E.5 PSSf Margin Adaptation

Projection‑to‑State Safety (PSSf) [4] quantifies safety under projected (state‑dependent) disturbances. For a system with disturbance d entering via a projection operator, the safe set shrinks by a margin proportional to the disturbance bound. Here, the stiffness uncertainty \Delta K_\phi acts as a projected disturbance on the joint dynamics. Following PSSf, we enlarge the HOCBF margin \epsilon by

\Delta\epsilon = \frac{\partial K_\phi}{\partial g} \frac{\sigma_g}{\kappa_j} q_j^{\max}.

With \sigma_g \approx 0.1 mm, the margin increase is less than 0.4°, negligible compared to the stroke limit. The enlarged margin ensures that the true (uncertain) system remains within the safe set with probability exceeding the confidence level derived from the EKF covariance.

