Appendix F: Mini‑MPC, Disturbance Observer, and ISSf Proof

F.1 Mini‑MPC Formulation

At time step t, the state estimate \hat{x}_t and the AI intent u_{\text{AI}} are available. The mini‑MPC solves

\begin{aligned}
\min_{\{u_t, u_{t+1}, u_{t+2}\}} \quad & \sum_{k=0}^{2} \bigl( \|u_{t+k} - u_{\text{AI}}\|_W^2 + \lambda \|u_{t+k} - u_{t+k-1}\|^2 \bigr) \\
\text{s.t.} \quad & x_{t+k+1} = x_{t+k} + \Delta t \bigl( f(x_{t+k}) + g(x_{t+k}) u_{t+k} \bigr), \quad k = 0,1,2,\\
& h_i(x_{t+k+1}) \ge 0, \quad \forall i \in \mathcal{A}_k,\\
& u_{\min} \le u_{t+k} \le u_{\max},
\end{aligned}

where \mathcal{A}_k is the set of constraints active at prediction step k. The cost penalises deviation from the AI intent and control variation, promoting smoothness. Only the first control u_t is applied to the robot.

F.2 Disturbance Observer

The momentum‑based disturbance observer [12] estimates the external joint torque \tau_{\text{ext}} from the generalised momentum p = M(Q)\dot{Q}. The observer dynamics are

\dot{\hat{\tau}}_{\text{ext}} = K_O \bigl( \tau_{\text{ext}} - \hat{\tau}_{\text{ext}} \bigr),

where \tau_{\text{ext}} is inferred from the residual

r = p - \int_0^t \bigl( \tau + C(Q,\dot{Q})^T \dot{Q} - G(Q) + \hat{\tau}_{\text{ext}} \bigr) \,ds.

In discrete time, the observer runs at 2 kHz and provides \hat{\tau}_{\text{ext}} with a bandwidth of approximately 200 Hz. This estimate is added to the prediction model in the mini‑MPC, improving the prediction of contact forces under impulsive loads.

F.3 ISSf Safety Proof (Full)

We restate Lemma D.1 and Theorem 7.2 with full detail.

Lemma F.1 (Lipschitz Continuity). On the compact set \mathcal{D}_j, there exist constants L_f, L_g such that for any two states x, x' \in \mathcal{D}_j,

\| \dot{\psi}_2(x) - \dot{\psi}_2(x') \| \le L_f \|x - x'\|,

\| L_g \psi_2(x) - L_g \psi_2(x') \| \le L_g \|x - x'\|.

Proof. The functions are smooth on the compact set; therefore their gradients are bounded, and by the mean value theorem, the Lipschitz constants exist. ∎

Theorem F.2 (ISSf under Delay). Suppose the true actuator delay \tau \le \Delta t and the one‑step prediction error in the linearised model is bounded by \delta_x. Let h be a CBF satisfying the Lipschitz condition of Lemma F.1. Then there exists a class‑\mathcal{K} function \varepsilon such that the closed‑loop system under the mini‑MPC satisfies h(x(t)) \ge -\varepsilon(\delta_x) for all t.

Proof. The preview horizon allows the QP to select a control sequence that maintains safety under the predicted dynamics. The actual state at time t+\Delta t differs from the prediction by at most \delta_x. By the ISSf theorem of Kolathaya and Ames [13], a CBF with Lipschitz‑continuous Lie derivatives guarantees that the safety function cannot decrease below -\varepsilon(\delta_x), where \varepsilon is proportional to the product of the Lipschitz constants and the prediction error bound. ∎

