Appendix G: Quadratic Programming Solver Architecture

G.1 qpSWIFT Solver Configuration

qpSWIFT is a sparse interior‑point active‑set solver optimised for small to medium‑sized QPs arising in real‑time control. The solver is compiled with double‑precision floating point and configured with a maximum of 100 iterations per solve; in practice, warm‑started problems terminate in 5–15 iterations. The Hessian matrix H is regularised with a small diagonal term 10^{-6} I to ensure positive definiteness.

G.2 Constraint Selection and Slack Hierarchy

The full set of constraints (50–80 rows) exceeds the computational budget of a 2 kHz loop. Constraint selection uses an urgency‑to‑cost scoring:

\text{score}(i) = \frac{-h_i(x)}{\| \text{intent deviation caused by constraint } i \| + \varepsilon},

with priority classes: CRITICAL (stroke, self‑collision, angular momentum bound), HIGH (DCM, friction cone), MEDIUM (CoP), LOW (auxiliary). Within each class, constraints are selected greedily until the total reaches |\mathcal{A}|_{\max} = 16. Redundant constraints (those dominated by a tighter constraint on the same variable) are pruned.

The slack variables \delta are partitioned by priority. The hard constraints (stroke, self‑collision) have \delta_k = 0 enforced; the slack penalty \rho for lower‑priority constraints is set to 10^4 to ensure they are violated only when physically unavoidable.

G.3 WCET Breakdown

The table in the main paper presents conservative upper bounds validated on a Jetson Thor (Ampere GPU, 8‑core ARM CPU). The CBF evaluation on GPU processes 50 constraints in parallel using 256 threads per block. The QP assembly (copying Jacobian rows from GPU to CPU and building the sparse matrix) is the primary bottleneck; it is optimised by pre‑allocating sparsity patterns and updating only the nonzero entries at each cycle.

G.4 Distributed Hardware Specification

· Central compute: NVIDIA Jetson Thor, 8‑core ARM Cortex‑A78AE, 2048‑core Ampere GPU, 32 GB LPDDR5.
· Coil drivers: 20× STM32G4 microcontrollers, each driving one full‑bridge MOSFET H‑bridge, communicating over EtherCAT at 100 Mbit/s.
· Photonic interrogator: Custom FPGA (Xilinx Artix‑7) measuring 32 differential waveguide channels at 10 kHz via transimpedance amplifiers and 16‑bit ADCs. Data are transferred to the central module via a dedicated 1 Gbit/s Ethernet link using a lightweight UDP protocol.
· EtherCAT master: Real‑time kernel module on the Jetson, synchronised with DC mode, 500 µs cycle time, 10 ms watchdog.

