Appendix H: Discrete‑Time Stroke Safety Proof

H.1 Euler Step Error Bound

The continuous‑time joint dynamics are

\ddot{q}_j = a(q_j, \dot{q}_j) + b(q_j) i_j,

with a = M_{\text{eff}}^{-1}( -K_\phi q_j - D_\phi \dot{q}_j + \tau_{\text{coupling},j}) and b = M_{\text{eff}}^{-1} \kappa_j. The Euler discretisation is

q_{k+1} = q_k + \Delta t \, \dot{q}_k,

\dot{q}_{k+1} = \dot{q}_k + \Delta t \, \bigl( a(q_k, \dot{q}_k) + b(q_k) i_k \bigr).

The local truncation error is O(\Delta t^2). Specifically, if \ddot{q} is bounded by \ddot{q}_{\max} (derived from the maximum coil current and the passive torque saturation), then

|q_{\text{true}}(t+\Delta t) - q_{\text{Euler}}| \le \frac{\Delta t^2}{2} \ddot{q}_{\max}.

With \Delta t = 500 µs and \ddot{q}_{\max} \approx 500 rad/s² (worst case near the stroke limit with maximum restoring torque), the error bound is 6.25 \times 10^{-5} rad \approx 0.0036^\circ, far smaller than the safety margin \epsilon \approx 1^\circ.

H.2 Proof of Theorem 9.1

Assume the HOCBF condition \dot{\psi}_2 + \gamma_3 \psi_2 \ge 0 holds for the Euler‑predicted state. By construction, this condition guarantees that the predicted \psi_2 does not decrease faster than allowed, hence the predicted state satisfies |q_{k+1}| \le q_j^{\max}. The true state differs from the prediction by at most the truncation error bound. Since the margin \epsilon exceeds this error bound by two orders of magnitude, the true state also satisfies the safety constraint. ∎

